# Skadi deploy stack configuration (SKADI-T-0064).
# Copy to .env (gitignored) and fill in. Every variable the compose file
# reads is listed here.

# --- VPN (gluetun) --------------------------------------------------------
# Provider name from gluetun's matrix: https://github.com/qdm12/gluetun-wiki
# e.g. nordvpn, mullvad, protonvpn, airvpn, ... or "custom" for raw configs.
VPN_SERVICE_PROVIDER=nordvpn
# Optional; defaults to openvpn. Set to wireguard to use the WG vars below.
#VPN_TYPE=openvpn

# OpenVPN (e.g. NordVPN): use the *service credentials* from your provider's
# dashboard (for Nord: Account -> NordVPN -> Manual setup), NOT your account
# login.
OPENVPN_USER=
OPENVPN_PASSWORD=

# Optional server selection (comma-separated), e.g. "United States" / "Chicago"
SERVER_COUNTRIES=
SERVER_CITIES=

# WireGuard alternative (set VPN_TYPE=wireguard, leave OpenVPN vars empty).
WIREGUARD_PRIVATE_KEY=
WIREGUARD_ADDRESSES=

# Inbound peer connections (SKADI-T-0587). With this off, nothing can connect
# *in* to the download worker, so a torrent whose only seeders sit behind their
# own NAT never starts (it stalls at 0% and gets blocklisted). Turn it on with a
# provider that forwards ports — protonvpn, pia, privatevpn, perfectprivacy —
# NordVPN does not offer it. The worker picks up gluetun's assigned port at
# startup; the vpn-watchdog restarts the worker on a tunnel bounce, so a
# re-assigned port is picked up too.
#VPN_PORT_FORWARDING=on
# Only for providers gluetun cannot infer (e.g. custom configs): protonvpn|pia|...
#VPN_PORT_FORWARDING_PROVIDER=

# --- Postgres -------------------------------------------------------------
POSTGRES_USER=skadi
# REQUIRED. Generate: openssl rand -hex 16
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=
POSTGRES_DB=skadi

# --- Skadi ----------------------------------------------------------------
# REQUIRED: bearer token for the HTTP API (auth is on by default here).
# Generate: openssl rand -hex 24
SKADI_API_TOKEN=

# REQUIRED: encrypts provider credentials at rest in the database.
# Generate: openssl rand -hex 32   (changing it later orphans stored secrets)
SKADI_SECRET_KEY=

# Optional: use TMDB directly for metadata instead of the keyless Servarr
# default (see ADR SKADI-A-0001 in .metis/adrs/).
SKADI_TMDB_API_KEY=

# --- Storage + skadi's library root ----------------------------------------
# The central WRITABLE storage (your NAS export) is mounted at /mnt/storage in
# BOTH skadi and the worker via the `storage` NFS volume. skadi writes ONLY under
# its dedicated library root `SKADI_LIBRARY_ROOT` (default /mnt/storage/skadi) — a
# skadi-only subfolder, so on a SHARED NAS it never touches your other folders.
# skadi owns the layout under ITS root, no root-folder registration:
#   /mnt/storage/skadi/movie  /mnt/storage/skadi/television  /mnt/storage/skadi/audiobook
#   /mnt/storage/skadi/downloads/complete   (torrents download + seed here)
# Everything lives under one NFS export so the importer HARDLINKS completed files
# instead of copying (the old separate DOWNLOADS_DIR + MEDIA_DIR mounts forced a
# cross-device copy that doubled disk and could fill the host).
#
# VERIFY this mount point before `docker compose up` — it is WRITABLE.
STORAGE_NFS_ADDR=<nas-ip>
STORAGE_NFS_PATH=/volume1/media
# skadi's library root: a DEDICATED, skadi-only subfolder of the mount. Create it
# empty on the NAS first. Change it only if you want a different skadi-only path.
SKADI_LIBRARY_ROOT=/mnt/storage/skadi

# --- Misc -----------------------------------------------------------------
# Uid/gid skadi and the worker run as (SKADI-T-0487). Leave both commented to
# use the image's own user, which is what every existing deployment already runs
# as: uid 1000, **gid 999** — `useradd --system` allocates the group from the low
# system range, so it is not 1000 and never was.
#
# Set them to match whoever owns your library if that tree is not world-writable.
# Overriding PUID also needs a writable HOME (`HOME=/tmp`), since /home/skadi
# belongs to uid 1000 inside the image — see docker-compose.nas.yml.
#PUID=1000
#PGID=999
TZ=Etc/UTC

# Concurrent CloudFlare solves (SKADI-T-0488). FlareSolverr drives one Chromium,
# so parallel challenges contend for it and each gets slower — a burst of twelve
# pushed them all past the healthcheck and autoheal killed the browser mid-solve.
# Set via the config API (`flaresolverr_max_concurrent`), default 2.

# --- Memory fuses (SKADI-T-0566) ------------------------------------------
# Caps for the two services that have no reason to grow without bound. These are
# fuses, not tuning: they turn a leak into one container restarting with an
# obvious cause instead of the host swapping. Measured 2026-09-10 on the Mac:
# worker ~535 MiB with 371 torrents, flaresolverr ~846 MiB steady / 993 MiB
# mid-solve. Raise them on a bigger host rather than editing the compose file.
#MEM_WORKER=1g
#MEM_FLARESOLVERR=1536m

# --- Database backups (SKADI-T-0481) --------------------------------------
# The postgres-backup sidecar dumps on a timer to the library share and keeps the
# newest N verified dumps. Defaults are daily / 14. See deploy/README
# "Database backup and restore".
#BACKUP_INTERVAL=86400
#BACKUP_KEEP=14
# Indexers are NATIVE (SKADI-I-0036): Skadi's built-in Cardigann engine replaces
# Prowlarr. Nothing to configure here — add trackers from the catalog in the UI
# (Indexers → "+ Add tracker"). The daemon's FlareSolverr/proxy/definitions wiring
# is set in docker-compose.yml (SKADI_FLARESOLVERR_URL / SKADI_CARDIGANN_*).
# The definition library auto-syncs from the Prowlarr/Indexers repo on boot + on a
# schedule (default daily, like Prowlarr). Optional overrides (defaults shown):
#   SKADI_CARDIGANN_SYNC_ENABLED=true
#   SKADI_CARDIGANN_SYNC_INTERVAL_SECS=86400
#   SKADI_CARDIGANN_DEFINITIONS_REVISION=master   # pin a commit/tag for repeatability
# Optional: FlareSolverr log level.
FLARESOLVERR_LOG_LEVEL=info

# --- Download worker (SKADI-I-0013) ---------------------------------------
# Worker config flows through the unified config plane (SKADI-I-0014): each
# SKADI_WORKER_* var is seeded into the `config` table on the worker's boot
# (env always wins) and read back via the typed config view. Names/defaults are
# unchanged; database_url stays direct env (it's how the worker reaches the table).
# Identifies the worker when it claims download jobs. One worker per stack.
SKADI_WORKER_ID=skadi-worker-1
# Pinned IP for Postgres on the compose network, so the worker (in gluetun's
# namespace, where compose service names don't resolve) can reach it. Must be
# inside COMPOSE_SUBNET and not collide with another fixed address.
POSTGRES_IP=172.28.0.5

# Host port for skadi's UI/API.
SKADI_PORT=8080
# How skadi's port is exposed on the host. Default 0.0.0.0 = reachable from
# the LAN (needed for the phone player: pairing, book + APK downloads). NOTE:
# on Docker Desktop 0.0.0.0 also binds VPN/Tailscale interfaces and IPv6, and
# the UI hands its API token to ANY visitor that reaches it — so keep this to
# your trusted home LAN and do NOT port-forward it through a router. To lock
# it back to this machine only, uncomment:
# SKADI_BIND=127.0.0.1
# Compose network subnet (must match FIREWALL_OUTBOUND_SUBNETS logic).
COMPOSE_SUBNET=172.28.0.0/16
RUST_LOG=info

# Upload cap in bytes/sec (SKADI-T-0516). Default 500000 (~500 KB/s): the box
# seeds back politely without saturating a home link, while staying well clear of
# silently capping the ratio a private tracker expects. `0` = unlimited. Download
# is uncapped by default. Also editable at runtime in Downloads -> Settings.
#SKADI_WORKER_UP_LIMIT_BPS=500000

# Route daemon egress through gluetun's HTTP proxy (SKADI-T-0522). Empty = direct.
# Cardigann searches already ride the VPN; without this, Torznab, metadata,
# notifiers and definition sync go direct — so a VPN meant to hide indexer
# traffic was leaking most of it. Loopback is always bypassed.
#SKADI_HTTP_PROXY_URL=http://gluetun:8888
#SKADI_HTTP_NO_PROXY=nas.local

# The host:port pairing QRs and install links advertise (SKADI-T-0486). Must be
# an address a phone on the LAN can reach — the container's own bind address is
# not it, so a QR generated without this points somewhere unreachable.
#SKADI_ADVERTISE_HOST=<host-ip>:8090

# Keep the embedded torrent client off the public DHT (SKADI-T-0486). Set for the
# lab, where every torrent is synthetic and announcing them is pure noise. Leave
# unset in production: DHT is how a public-tracker torrent finds peers.
#SKADI_WORKER_DISABLE_DHT=1

# How a completed download is placed into the library (SKADI-T-0138).
#   seed  (default) hardlink and keep the source, so the torrent keeps seeding.
#         On a single shared export this costs no extra disk — both names share
#         an inode — and it is the ratio-friendly, *arr-standard behaviour.
#   move  remove the source once the library copy is placed, for an operator who
#         does not seed and wants the download directory to stay clean.
# Applies to the acquire path only; library-import always hardlinks, because its
# source is your existing library and must never be moved.
#SKADI_IMPORT_PLACEMENT=seed

# --- Remote access via Tailscale (SKADI-T-0599) ---------------------------
# Optional. Joins the daemon to your tailnet so the Android app (and anything
# else on the tailnet) can reach it away from home: pair the app with
# http://<TAILSCALE_HOSTNAME>:8080 (MagicDNS) or http://<tailnet IP>:8080.
# 1. Tailscale admin console → Settings → Keys → Generate auth key (a one-off
#    key is fine; state persists in a volume). Put it here.
# 2. Set COMPOSE_PROFILES=tailscale and run `angreal deploy up`.
# 3. In the admin console, disable key expiry for the new "skadi" node.
# 4. Install Tailscale on the phone, sign in, then pair the app as above.
#TAILSCALE_AUTHKEY=tskey-auth-...
#TAILSCALE_HOSTNAME=skadi
# Extra `tailscale up` flags, e.g. --advertise-tags=tag:skadi
#TAILSCALE_EXTRA_ARGS=
# Compose profiles to enable (comma-separated). `tailscale` starts the node.
#COMPOSE_PROFILES=tailscale
